## Issues with This Infrastructure

### 1. Terminating SSL at the Load Balancer Is an Issue

When SSL is terminated at the load balancer:
- Traffic between the load balancer and backend servers travels **unencrypted (HTTP)**
- If an attacker gains access to the internal network, they can intercept plaintext traffic between the load balancer and servers
- This is a risk in environments where the internal network is not trusted (e.g., multi-tenant data centers, cloud environments)
- **Solution:** Use SSL passthrough (end-to-end encryption) or re-encrypt traffic from load balancer to servers (SSL bridging)

### 2. Only One MySQL Server Capable of Accepting Writes

The Primary-Replica setup means only the Primary accepts write operations. This creates:
- **A write SPOF:** If the Primary fails, no writes can occur until the Replica is promoted — a manual process that takes time
- **A write bottleneck:** All write traffic is funneled to one server regardless of load
- **Replication lag risk:** If the Primary fails before replication completes, the Replica may have stale data — causing data loss
- **Solution:** Implement multi-primary clustering (e.g., Galera Cluster) or automated failover (e.g., MySQL Group Replication)

### 3. Servers with All the Same Components Is a Problem

Running web server + application server + database on every machine causes:
- **Resource contention:** The database competes with the web server and app server for RAM and CPU on the same machine — each component underperforms
- **Security blast radius:** A compromise of the web layer (most exposed) immediately gives access to the database layer on the same machine
- **Scaling inflexibility:** You cannot scale the database independently of the web server — adding a server adds all components, even if only one is the bottleneck
- **Maintenance complexity:** Upgrading or restarting any single component risks affecting the others on the same host
- **Solution:** Separate components onto dedicated servers (as shown in Task 3)
